  ## Check 4 — real test run — PASS

  ```
  $ npx vitest run overrides/app/components/product-item/index.test.tsx

   RUN  v3.2.7 /Users/marcel.a.n.navarro/Dev/zimmermann-pwa

  stderr | ... > renders the image with an alt, the name uppercase, the price, and one combined colour+size node
  Could not parse CSS stylesheet
  stderr | ... > hideStepperAndActions suppresses the stepper and every actions surface, and nothing throws
  Could not parse CSS stylesheet
  stderr | ... > every existing caller (hideStepperAndActions omitted) is unaffected — the stepper and actions still render
  Could not parse CSS stylesheet

   ✓ overrides/app/components/product-item/index.test.tsx (3 tests) 169ms

   Test Files  1 passed (1)
        Tests  3 passed (3)
     Start at  15:47:27
     Duration  2.03s
  ```
  All 3 tests pass. "Could not parse CSS stylesheet" is a benign jsdom/emotion stderr warning, not a test failure.

  ## Overall verdict: CONFIRMED

  Claim 4 holds up under independent verification, including the specific two-render-site trap the task flagged as most likely to be missed. `hideStepperAndActions` is correctly typed (`?: boolean`, defaults `false`), and gates every render site in the component — critically, **both** `deliveryActions` sites (`index.tsx:174-176` and `:179-181`), not just one — plus both `primaryAction` sites and the single `secondaryActions`/stepper sites. The test file proves this with real DOM-absence assertions (not just "doesn't throw"), and I confirmed via `responsive/index.tsx` that `HideOnMobile`/`HideOnDesktop` always mount both children in the DOM (CSS-only hiding), which means the test's singular `queryByText`/`queryByRole` checks would necessarily fail (via assertion failure or a Testing-Library multi-match throw) had only one of the two `deliveryActions`/`primaryAction` sites been gated — this is a real, working trap, not an accidental pass. A separate test proves every existing caller (prop omitted) renders unaffected, with positive presence assertions correctly split singular/plural per slot. The `GiftCard` story exists and uses the prop. The real vitest run passes 3/3. One non-blocking observation: the `GiftCard` story (and its adjacent `index.stories.test.tsx` counterpart) omits `deliveryActions` from its args, so neither demonstrates that specific slot being suppressed visually — that coverage lives only in `index.test.tsx`, which does cover it correctly.
